BEGIN;

UPDATE public.mock_test_questions SET explanation = E'Option A (correct) is EC2 instances in cluster placement group with FSx for Lustre shared storage. This combination optimizes HPC performance by providing dedicated compute resources with low-latency inter-node communication and a high-performance parallel file system. EC2 instances can be provisioned with powerful compute-optimized or GPU instances to meet high processing demands. A cluster placement group ensures instances are launched within a single Availability Zone on hardware that supports low-latency network connectivity and high network throughput, which is crucial for tightly coupled parallel computing. FSx for Lustre is a high-performance parallel file system specifically designed for HPC workloads, offering very high throughput and low-latency access to shared datasets, making it ideal for processing large datasets across multiple compute nodes simultaneously.

Option B (incorrect) is ECS with Fargate and EFS for shared file system access, which is not optimal for extreme HPC. Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers, abstracting away the underlying infrastructure. While convenient, it typically doesn''t offer the same level of granular control over networking or the guaranteed low-latency interconnect that dedicated EC2 instances in a cluster placement group provide, which is critical for tightly coupled HPC applications. EFS is a shared file system but generally provides higher latency and lower aggregate throughput compared to FSx for Lustre, especially for the demanding I/O patterns of large-scale scientific computing applications.

Option C (incorrect) is Lambda functions with S3 for data storage and parallel processing. Lambda functions are serverless, event-driven compute services suitable for highly parallelizable, stateless tasks with short execution times. They are not designed for workloads requiring low-latency inter-node communication, long-running processes, or the ability to directly mount a high-speed shared file system, all of which are common in HPC. S3 is object storage, excellent for data durability and scalability, but it is not a file system and does not provide the low-latency, high-throughput file access semantics necessary for active processing of large datasets by compute nodes in an HPC cluster.

Option D (incorrect) is Batch processing with EBS volumes and instance store for temporary data. While AWS Batch can manage HPC workloads, the storage combination is fundamentally unsuitable for shared high-speed storage across an HPC cluster. EBS volumes are block storage attached to a single EC2 instance (with limited multi-attach functionality, but not a parallel file system). They cannot be simultaneously accessed by multiple compute nodes as a shared file system with the required performance. Instance store provides very high I/O performance but is ephemeral (data is lost upon instance termination or stop) and is local to a single instance, meaning it cannot provide shared storage across the compute nodes of a cluster.' WHERE id = '44644889-3a30-49a8-9f45-008337a56396'; -- CORRECTED EXPLANATION
